ked it out I  began supplementing preceding and  sometimes even replacing my lectures  with your videos and I received an 84%  on my first midterm she this is the  third time he's taking it I almost cried  I was so happy  and you know I'd and when I when I first  try 84 that's a good score why is he  crying especially because the class  average was a 47%  [Applause]  now the second midterm is coming up I  feel confident enough to say that I will  most likely get an A in the class since  my major depends on me passing the class  I want to thank you for helping me save  my college career so it's pretty you  know a mind mind mind blowing stuff and  I think I think I think you can imagine  why I quit my day job as a hedge fund  manager what wasn't wasn't quite as much  psychic reward but I think the takeaway  here is is that and this is the learning  that we have this thing that we never  want to forget as an organization is  that people come to the Khan Academy  wanting to just fill in the gap they  have an exam they want to pass an exam  but once they actually experience what  it's like to learn it doesn't matter who  they are it could be the students who's  about to drop out once they actually  experience it for the first time of life  they get addicted it's a natural hide  it's it's something that we innately all  humans want to have and that's something  as an organization we don't want to ever  forget that if we can form these human  bonds using technology that's what's  actually going to allow us to succeed  and so I want to leave you with one  quick a little story from from one of  the Los Altos classrooms last week that  kind of hits hits the point home there  was a reporter from NBC doing a story on  what we're doing in that classroom and  there's a little fifth grader and she's  doing trigonometry and actually all of  the boys are very envious um because she  has the most badges and points and all  of that other's things and the reporter  sits down next to her and says do you  think this is 5th grade math and then  the little girl has a mischievous smile  and she looks at the reporter she goes  no I think it's sixth grade thank you  [Applause]
 

